  "notes": "Deliberately maximal \"hick-hop-adjacent\" party country — rap-cadence verses, gospel choir choruses, novelty spectacle. Drums encode the stated hybrid: \"the party cuts use a hip-hop-informed programmed layer (claps, 16th shaker, sampled hits) *over* a live kit, with breakdown sections that drop to percussion-and-vocal only. Ballads are conventional cross-stick builds\" — so the snare is layered with a clap, the shaker runs 16ths and the drop-out figure is high. Harmony is \"I–IV–V with a I–♭VII rock vamp and gospel IV–I plagal cadences,\" all three authored. The melody is the entry's most specific claim and the reason this model's density ceiling is the highest in the batch: \"rap-cadenced spoken-sung verses with high syllable density (6–8 syllables per beat in places), then a wide, chanted chorus — the verse/chorus contrast is deliberately extreme.\" ⚠ The research describes \"a programmed sub reinforcing beats 1 and 3\" *under* the live bass — reinforcement, not the low end — so no `drums.bass808` block is authored and the model inherits the genre's live bass. Authoring an 808 in the `bassline` role here would delete the live bass part the entry describes. ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the clap offsets, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
